<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Konark Sun Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1250</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Konark Sun Temple is a 13th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple to the sun god Surya at Konark on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built around 1250 by the Eastern Ganga king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhadeva-i">Narasimhadeva I</a>. It is designed as a giant stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> with twenty-four carved wheels drawn by seven horses. Its main tower has collapsed, but the surviving hall was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konark-sun-temple.jpg" alt="Konark Sun Temple" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Subham9423 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konarka_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Konark, called Kainapara in Indian texts, was a significant trading port by the early centuries of the common era, and a sun temple is thought to have stood in the area by at least the 9th century. The present temple is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhadeva-i">Narasimhadeva I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a>, who reigned from 1238 to 1264. Planning and construction records written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in the Odia script survive on palm leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> found in a village in the 1960s, which is unusual for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple. The king sponsored the work, and Shiva Samantaraya Mahapatra oversaw it.</p><p>It was built near an older Surya temple, whose sanctum sculpture was re-consecrated in the new temple. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper-plate-inscriptions">Copper plate inscriptions</a> of the era call the Konark temple the great cottage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple takes the form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> of Surya, who in Vedic iconography rides across the sky behind seven horses, driven by the charioteer Aruna. The seven horses are named after the seven metres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> prosody, and the chariot's twelve pairs of wheels correspond to the twelve months of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> calendar. The wheels are nearly 3.7 metres across and also serve as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundial">sundials</a>. The temple faces east, so that the first rays of the sunrise strike its entrance. It was built from three kinds of stone: chlorite, laterite and khondalite.</p><p>The complex had a main sanctuary, the deul, which was estimated at some 70 metres high, and in front of it the jagamohana or assembly hall, about 39 metres tall, which still stands. The dance hall, the Nata mandira, and the dining hall also survive. Its walls carry reliefs of deities, musicians, animals, scenes of daily life and couples in erotic scenes. A pillar to Aruna that stood before the eastern stairs was moved in the last quarter of the 18th century to the Lion's Gate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a> temple in Puri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Why the temple fell is uncertain. An inscription of 1384 shows it was complete and in worship, and a text of the 16th century describes it as prosperous, with no mention of ruins. Some attribute damage to attacks by Muslim armies between the 15th and 17th centuries, among them one by the general Kalapahar. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> holy man later found it abandoned and overgrown. The main tower collapsed, leaving the sanctum without a roof.</p><p>Removal of stones was forbidden in 1803, but later some stones and sculptures were taken to Puri and, in 1894, thirteen sculptures to the Indian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. In 1903 the jagamohana was sealed and filled with sand to prevent its collapse. The Mayadevi temple was discovered in 1909, and the temple was made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1984. European sailors had called it the Black Pagoda from as early as 1676.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Konark Sun Temple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Narasimhadeva I built the <b>Sun Temple</b> at Konark around 1250 and fought the Muslim rulers of Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-ganga-dynasty">Eastern Ganga dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
